      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "In order to be \"clock equivalent\" to its square, a number n must have a remainder of 1 or 7 when divided by 12.\nThat is to say that n can be written as $12k+1$ or $12k+7$, for some other whole number k.\nWe know that n must be greater than 4.\nSo, if we divide 4 by 12, we get 4 = 12•0 + 4, which does not work. We see that $12•1 + 1$ works because 4 ÷ 12 = 0 remainder 4 → (12•1 + 1) ÷ 12 = 1 remainder 1. We can also see that $12•1+7$ works because 4 ÷ 12 = 0 remainder 4 → (12•1 + 7) ÷ 12 = 1 remainder 7 Therefore, the least whole number n > 4 that is \"clock equivalent\" to its square number of hours is 13.\nAs you can (or should) test, the squares of these numbers > 4 are clock equivalent, so 13 is the smallest one. PRODUCED BY UNWARNING SOLUTIONS, INC.\nThe answer is 13",
